                "**Hypodermic Needle / Magic Bullet Model (1920s–30s)**: Media 'injects' messages directly into a passive audience with immediate, powerful effects — originated from concerns about Nazi propaganda and early mass media; largely discredited as over-simplistic but still underlies media censorship debates.\n  • 💡 *Simple Explanation*: In plain words, this means how hypodermic needle / magic bullet model (1920s–30s) works when you look at it in real life.\n  • 🔍 *Real-World Analogy*: Picture this like an everyday system where every part has one specific job to do.\n  • ⚠️ *Common Mistake*: Students often mix this up with related ideas—make sure to mention both *what* it is and *why* it matters.",
                "**Two-Step Flow Model (Katz & Lazarsfeld, 1955)**: Media messages reach audiences via 'opinion leaders' who interpret and disseminate them within social networks — better accounts for selective influence; social media influencers are modern opinion leaders.\n  • 💡 *Simple Explanation*: In plain words, this means how two-step flow model (katz & lazarsfeld, 1955) works when you look at it in real life.\n  • 🔍 *Real-World Analogy*: Picture this like an everyday system where every part has one specific job to do.\n  • ⚠️ *Common Mistake*: Students often mix this up with related ideas—make sure to mention both *what* it is and *why* it matters.",
                "**Uses and Gratifications Theory (Blumler & Katz, 1974)**: Audiences actively choose media to satisfy specific needs — **Information/Surveillance** (news), **Personal identity** (comparing self to media representations), **Social interaction** (parasocial relationships with characters/celebrities), **Entertainment/Escapism**. Shifts focus from media effects TO audience agency.\n  • 💡 *Simple Explanation*: In plain words, this means how uses and gratifications theory (blumler & katz, 1974) works when you look at it in real life.\n  • 🔍 *Real-World Analogy*: Picture this like an everyday system where every part has one specific job to do.\n  • ⚠️ *Common Mistake*: Students often mix this up with related ideas—make sure to mention both *what* it is and *why* it matters.",
                "**Reception Theory / Encoding–Decoding (Stuart Hall, 1980)**: Producers encode texts with a preferred meaning; audiences decode them — but not necessarily in the intended way. Three positions: **Dominant/Preferred reading** (accepts the intended meaning); **Negotiated reading** (partially accepts, partially resists); **Oppositional reading** (rejects the preferred meaning from an alternative ideological position).\n  • 💡 *Simple Explanation*: In plain words, this means how reception theory / encoding–decoding (stuart hall, 1980) works when you look at it in real life.\n  • 🔍 *Real-World Analogy*: Picture this like an everyday system where every part has one specific job to do.\n  • ⚠️ *Common Mistake*: Students often mix this up with related ideas—make sure to mention both *what* it is and *why* it matters.",
